 ## Background
-The concept of multi-resolution rendering for volumes originated with the need to interactively visualize large medical and scientific datasets [G]. Early work by Laur and Hanrahan (1991) introduced hierarchical point clouds using error metrics [G]. Subsequent developments extended these ideas to volumetric splatting using pyramidal structures [G] and more recently to 3D Gaussian Splatting to handle the memory constraints of neural radiance fields [G]. The technique is widely used in scientific visualization, medical imaging, and real-time rendering of large-scale 3D scenes [G].
